        "raw": "% ATTEMPT_STATUS: unresolved\n\\section*{Random axes and the limits of angular reweighting}\n\\noindent\\textbf{Problem ID:} \\texttt{D82-151}. \\textbf{Status: unresolved.}\n\n\\section{Target and scope}\nThe target is the best worst-case expected Euclidean social-cost ratio over\nall universally truthful planar facility mechanisms, uniformly in the\npopulation size. Barak \\cite[Sections 2--4]{Barak} establishes $4/\\pi$ for\nrandomly rotated coordinate medians and leaves the unrestricted optimum\nopen. We give a self-contained upper-bound proof and a minimax reduction:\nchanging the report-independent distribution of axes cannot improve the\nworst-case ratio of a fixed coordinate-median rule. This is a restriction\non a proposed direction of improvement, not a lower bound for every\nuniversally truthful mechanism.\n\nFor $n\\ge2$ set $k=\\lfloor(n+1)/2\\rfloor$. The deterministic rule $C_n$\nreturns the $k$th order statistic in each of the two coordinates; in\nparticular it uses the lower median when $n$ is even. Let $R_\\theta$\ndenote rotation through $\\theta$, and define\n\\[\n C_{n,\\theta}(x)=R_{-\\theta}C_n(R_\\theta x_1,\\ldots,R_\\theta x_n).\n\\]\nAll random angles below are independent of the reports. These maps are\nBorel measurable since order statistics are continuous.\n\n\\section{Truthfulness and the average norm bound}\n\\begin{lemma}[The one-dimensional option interval]\\label{clamp}\nFix $n-1$ real reports and an order rank $1\\le k\\le n$. The $k$th\norder statistic after inserting a report $z$ is the projection of $z$\nonto a fixed closed interval, allowing an infinite endpoint. Consequently,\nfor every true location $a$, its truthful output minimizes distance from\n$a$ over all obtainable outputs.\n\\end{lemma}\n\\begin{proof}\nOrder the other reports as $b_1\\le\\cdots\\le b_{n-1}$, set\n$b_0=-\\infty,b_n=+\\infty$, and inspect the three cases\n$z\\le b_{k-1}$, $b_{k-1}\\le z\\le b_k$, and $z\\ge b_k$.\nThe output is respectively $b_{k-1},z,b_k$, with the evident interpretation\nat infinite endpoints. This is exactly projection onto $[b_{k-1},b_k]$.\nProjection is the closest point in that interval to $a$.\n\\end{proof}\n\\begin{theorem}\\label{upper}\nEvery $C_{n,\\theta}$ is truthful for Euclidean distance and unanimous.\nFor uniform $\\Theta$ on $[0,2\\pi)$, the resulting mechanism has finite\nexpected costs and satisfies, for every profile $x$,\n\\[\n {\\mathbb{E}}{\\operatorname{SC}}(x,C_{n,\\Theta}(x))\\le\\frac4\\pi{\\operatorname{OPT}}(x).\n\\]\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nIn the rotated coordinate system, Lemma~\\ref{clamp} shows that a truthful\nreport weakly minimizes each of the two absolute coordinate errors.\nSquaring and adding these two inequalities proves Euclidean truthfulness;\nrotations preserve distances. Unanimity follows immediately.\n\nWrite $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$ for the sum of absolute coordinates. Coordinate medians\nminimize $\\sum_i\\|R_\\theta x_i-z\\|_1$: for a scalar coordinate, the slope\nof the convex sum of absolute deviations changes from nonpositive to\nnonnegative on the median interval. Thus, for a Euclidean minimizer $y^*$,\n\\begin{align*}\n {\\operatorname{SC}}(x,C_{n,\\theta}(x))\n &\\le\\sum_i\\|R_\\theta x_i-C_n(R_\\theta x)\\|_1\\\\\n &\\le\\sum_i\\|R_\\theta(x_i-y^*)\\|_1.\n\\end{align*}\nA minimizer exists because the social cost is continuous and tends to\ninfinity when $\\|y\\|_2$ tends to infinity. For every fixed vector $v$,\n\\[\n \\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\|R_\\theta v\\|_1\\,d\\theta\n =\\|v\\|_2\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\n (|\\cos\\theta|+|\\sin\\theta|)\\,d\\theta\n =\\frac4\\pi\\|v\\|_2.\n\\]\nIntegrating the preceding pointwise inequality proves the result and\nfiniteness. At unanimous profiles both sides are zero.\n\\end{proof}\nThis proof is a reconstruction of the standard random-projection norm\nargument underlying the cited bound, not a claim of a new upper bound.\n\n\\section{A minimax obstruction to choosing better axes}\nFor a Borel probability measure $\\nu$ on the circle define\n\\[\n W_n(\\nu)=\\sup_{x:{\\operatorname{OPT}}(x)>0}\n \\frac{\\int{\\operatorname{SC}}(x,C_{n,\\theta}(x))\\,d\\nu(\\theta)}{{\\operatorname{OPT}}(x)}.\n\\]\nLet $u$ be uniform angular measure. The next assertion does not assume\nthat $\\nu$ has a density or finite support.\n\\begin{theorem}[Uniform angular mixing is minimax]\\label{minimax}\nFor every $n\\ge2$ and every $\\nu$, $W_n(\\nu)\\ge W_n(u)$.\nConsequently, for arbitrary population-dependent measures $(\\nu_n)$,\n\\[\n \\sup_{n\\ge2}W_n(\\nu_n)\\ge\\sup_{n\\ge2}W_n(u).\n\\]\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nFix a nonunanimous profile $x$ and let\n$h_x(\\theta)={\\operatorname{SC}}(x,C_{n,\\theta}(x))/{\\operatorname{OPT}}(x)$.\nThe identity\n\\[\n C_{n,\\theta}(R_\\varphi x)=R_\\varphi C_{n,\\theta+\\varphi}(x)\n\\]\nfollows by multiplying the rotations. Both social cost and the optimum\nare rotation-invariant, so the ratio on the rotated profile is\n$\\int h_x(\\theta+\\varphi)\\,d\\nu(\\theta)$. Every such value is at most\n$W_n(\\nu)$. Integrate over uniform $\\varphi$. Tonelli's theorem applies\nto the nonnegative integrand, and translation-invariance of uniform\nmeasure gives\n\\[\n W_n(\\nu)\\ge\n \\int\\!\\int h_x(\\theta+\\varphi)\\,du(\\varphi)d\\nu(\\theta)\n =\\int h_x(\\psi)\\,du(\\psi).\n\\]\nTaking the supremum over $x$ proves the first assertion, and taking the\nsupremum over $n$ proves the second.\n\\end{proof}\nThe argument applies to any fixed measurable base mechanism and its\nrotation conjugates whenever its cost ratio is defined. Thus it is more\nthan a statement about discrete angle grids. Any strict improvement over\nthe uniform rotation guarantee must change the base family, use additional\nmechanisms, or exploit structure other than reweighting absolute axes.\n\n\\section{The missing unrestricted lower bound}\nFor completeness, fixed report-independent mixtures of truthful rules\nremain universally truthful by their definition as distributions over\nseeds; Theorem~\\ref{minimax} covers only mixtures of the displayed orbit.\nIt does not compare this orbit with a mixture of distinct base rules.\nNor does it allow the angle distribution to depend on reports: such a\nchoice requires a fresh incentive proof.\n\nThe cited source proves tightness of its random-rotation mechanism. We\nhave not reproved that lower-bound construction here, and we do not infer\nfrom it that the global optimum is $4/\\pi$. The precise remaining task\nis a lower bound that applies to every deterministic truthful rule used\nas a seed, or a universally truthful construction outside the above orbit\nwith a smaller uniform worst-case ratio. No finite computation is used.\nThe HTML source was consulted on 9 October 2026, including the universal\ntruthfulness statement and the planar upper bound.\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{Barak} Z. Barak, \\emph{Facility Location Mechanism Design:\nBreaking The Deterministic Barrier}, arXiv:2605.24750v2, 2026.\nSections 2--4, especially the future-directions paragraph and Theorem 4.\n\\url{https://arxiv.org/html/2605.24750v2}.\n\\end{thebibliography}",
